ef 17592186423381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423438 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bebafe40-b7bb-506e-8e35-eb97971e464b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423439 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c5350519-c626-566f-a1a4-6e0887abe2f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423440 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f1c5df89-0a4d-58f0-9562-de84c7296eba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186423427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186423392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423441 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f747b354-6e66-5b1a-8d1f-bb7fe1e6ed70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161216" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d3/d333ab14f8a42bb1d40894421afc76ff60acbb6c4cb858ddee5f6e8a39be2d15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0aa0eff9-8245-5a13-9648-df63821931fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423442 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/51/5193451b39b03b11cf9780fd7039360ba8fb5de5876a3593f7a5dadde2e7be5b.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-1}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-0}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-8\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/07/0785d19e62e5b317bbaee924fa8b6b303c2bbd10ab338357e6a88846b1d5b1d1.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "348cfda2-2f1c-590f-874e-27ed4639164d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423443 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fd/fdaad0039f4b7fea5c6e301f4cc3b89687f5f4081e3bd87f8ce31daacda2744b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}9-6y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[9y-3{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}-yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2}9\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-9[y]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6+(-18) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162541" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/58/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "541f1701-1958-5d60-973d-d311e60b511f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423444 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/00/0072a53c42916c095c625cebd0a0564b92dcac5469f9d7452e9c75ef7e7d7158.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=3$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨(3-3){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨(3-x){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3-0){e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({e}^{4}-1) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}) \\\\  & =3\\pi (1-{e}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/83/838fe13d4ad778c391ff628b508d17ed450b56e800180c946038be1ccfc5655e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5dff93a1-4655-5852-a4ef-0e50149062ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423445 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/d4/d47102fe8d01acf93fe0e6ef9017e06412e843ea64584b9f4e7151214aeb9197.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+6⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x-y+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-y+6\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy+6x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{13}{2}-y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{13}{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y+2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}y-x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y-x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}y-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =11+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4a/4af0d5c96e08d87ea884ec1f0d5c87e0ca220c54c2783fb299c100cad6582088.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74852653-8123-54e4-8cee-cb7081e03f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423446 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/e4/e4488f9fbf8c16883ac2470544d5fadcb63b2bff362b25a41a8a70bfe48232a2.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨8{x}^{2}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8({1}^{2})({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}8({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =8{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{∬}_{D}({y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =8{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =64\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨8({0}^{2})({y}^{2}+{z}^{2}),\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =64\\pi +0 \\\\  & =64\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-y,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ea6dcfa-a1e9-5981-812c-443a861d4f35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423447 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨-y,\\,x,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =-xy+xy+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "886facf5-e284-5b49-82f5-9671e671cb20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423448 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/e2/e2d914cf61af684d1f2971ec680acc1a2a53a6a40c85e387728dfea8ebc94730.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x-2+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =x+2{z}^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+(2{z}^{2}-2)x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2{z}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,x-y+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,x+2{z}^{2},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(x+2{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(x+2{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x{z}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{2}z+\\frac{2}{3}{z}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{3}{2}+18) \\\\  & =-\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}+(-\\frac{39}{2}) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c176acf-06da-52d5-8022-73333152fefe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423449 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-z,\\,y,\\,z+x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161220" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e8708a16a0f6db347acf2c1a82a602d2fa8ce60d4bb8369f1414eca3465e4e71.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b265113a-f521-5f72-933b-1132d7a6f831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423450 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8e/8ed6ffdeab54bc6888d245ef4f1c050c31e010c67dfa93aea365fc6cb523e382.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy-4x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y-3\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-3y]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}2x+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{15}{2}) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6c92f89-f1d7-5cd5-9f3c-fab3133854ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423451 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-3z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+3z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-3xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cea56a0f-1b32-5284-84a0-25dd8320e070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423452 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =x(3x+4y)+y(3y-4x)+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+4xy+3{y}^{2}-4xy+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186423434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/14b1144f52dcacc094901a159bf54c04f00daccc85df7b117b2163ed327dcd4f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec4a413a-1560-589f-a588-2fce65f680ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186423453 #edb/ref 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/61/614037b52ca1f60f03420019891af1004227e2e0f3b4a89d71adbdc68dadf1dd.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=2$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(y,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for ${y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(0,y,z)=⟨0\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,0,\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
